"use strict";(globalThis.webpackChunkp_vs_np_docs=globalThis.webpackChunkp_vs_np_docs||[]).push([[4385],{56488(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"tag":{"label":"complexity-zoo","permalink":"/azeon/p-vs-np/tags/complexity-zoo","allTagsPath":"/azeon/p-vs-np/tags","count":10,"items":[{"id":"complexity-zoo/chess-and-go-are-in-exp","title":"Chess and Go Are in EXP","description":"Blow chess and Go up to an n \xd7 n board and deciding who wins with perfect play becomes EXP-Complete, provably outside P. So how does the app on my phone beat me every time?","permalink":"/azeon/p-vs-np/complexity-zoo/chess-and-go-are-in-exp"},{"id":"complexity-zoo/exp-exponential-time","title":"EXP: Exponential Time","description":"Finally, a separation we can actually prove. The Time Hierarchy Theorem says that more time really does let you solve more problems, and it pins down a class of problems that are guaranteed not to be in P.","permalink":"/azeon/p-vs-np/complexity-zoo/exp-exponential-time"},{"id":"complexity-zoo/np-hard-vs-np-complete","title":"NP-Hard vs. NP-Complete","description":"Two labels I\'d been using almost interchangeably. NP-Complete means \'hardest inside NP\'. NP-Hard only means \'at least that hard\', and some NP-Hard problems can\'t be solved by any computer, ever.","permalink":"/azeon/p-vs-np/complexity-zoo/np-hard-vs-np-complete"},{"id":"complexity-zoo/quantum-computing-and-bqp","title":"Quantum Computing and BQP","description":"A quantum computer is not a computer that tries every answer at once. It\'s a computer whose possibilities can cancel each other out. That one idea, interference, is what defines the class BQP.","permalink":"/azeon/p-vs-np/complexity-zoo/quantum-computing-and-bqp"},{"id":"complexity-zoo/randomized-algorithms-bpp","title":"Randomized Algorithms (BPP)","description":"What if an algorithm could flip coins, and was allowed to be wrong one time in a trillion? Some problems seem to get much easier. Whether randomness really adds power is open, and most researchers think the answer is no.","permalink":"/azeon/p-vs-np/complexity-zoo/randomized-algorithms-bpp"},{"id":"complexity-zoo/shors-algorithm","title":"Shor\'s Algorithm","description":"How a quantum computer would factor huge numbers in polynomial time. Most of the algorithm is ordinary arithmetic you can do by hand. The quantum part is exactly one step: finding when a repeating pattern starts over.","permalink":"/azeon/p-vs-np/complexity-zoo/shors-algorithm"},{"id":"complexity-zoo/tautologies-and-conp-completeness","title":"Tautologies and coNP-Completeness","description":"A tautology is a formula that\'s true no matter what. Deciding whether a formula is one is the hardest problem in coNP, and it\'s tied to an old question: does every true statement have a short proof?","permalink":"/azeon/p-vs-np/complexity-zoo/tautologies-and-conp-completeness"},{"id":"complexity-zoo/the-class-conp","title":"The Class coNP","description":"NP is about problems where a yes has a short proof. Flip it, and you get coNP, where a no has a short proof. It sounds like a small twist. It turns out to be its own unsolved problem, sitting right next to P vs NP.","permalink":"/azeon/p-vs-np/complexity-zoo/the-class-conp"},{"id":"complexity-zoo/the-class-pspace","title":"The Class PSPACE","description":"Time can\'t be reused. Memory can. That one difference creates a class of problems that might take forever to solve but never need much room, and it\'s where two-player games live.","permalink":"/azeon/p-vs-np/complexity-zoo/the-class-pspace"},{"id":"complexity-zoo/will-quantum-computers-solve-np","title":"Will Quantum Computers Solve NP?","description":"Probably not. There\'s a quantum algorithm that speeds up brute-force search, and a theorem that says, in an important sense, it\'s the best possible. A square root of exponential is still exponential.","permalink":"/azeon/p-vs-np/complexity-zoo/will-quantum-computers-solve-np"}],"unlisted":false}}')}}]);